====================================================================
           DOCKER WORKFLOW GUIDE FOR AMJ-EXFINDER
====================================================================

1. DOCKER CONCEPTS OVERVIEW
--------------------------------------------------------------------
• Dockerfile : A text blueprint containing instructions on how to build
               your application environment (Python version, system 
               libraries, and application files).
• Docker Image: The compiled, read-only package generated from your 
               Dockerfile. Think of it as a template or snapshot.
• Container   : A live, running instance of your Docker Image operating 
               in an isolated environment.


2. STEP-BY-STEP COMMAND WORKFLOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1: Navigate to Project Directory
Ensure terminal is opened where 'Dockerfile' and 'app.py' exist.
$ cd /path/to/AMJ-EXfinder

Step 2: Build the Docker Image
Creates an image named 'amj-exfinder' tagged as 'latest'.
$ docker build -t amj-exfinder:latest .

Step 3: Run the Container from the Image
Starts the container in background mode (-d), mapping port 5000 of your 
host machine to port 5000 inside the container.
$ docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name amj_exfinder_app amj-exfinder:latest

Step 4: Verify Application Status
Access your browser at http://localhost:5000 or run:
$ docker ps


3. USEFUL CONTAINER MANAGEMENT COMMANDS
--------------------------------------------------------------------
• View Application Logs (Stream live logs):
  $ docker logs -f amj_exfinder_app

• Stop the Running Container:
  $ docker stop amj_exfinder_app

• Start a Stopped Container:
  $ docker start amj_exfinder_app

• Restart the Container:
  $ docker restart amj_exfinder_app

• Access Container Terminal (Inside Debian/Linux environment):
  $ docker exec -it amj_exfinder_app /bin/bash

• Remove Container Completely:
  $ docker rm -f amj_exfinder_app

• Remove Built Image:
  $ docker rmi amj-exfinder:latest
====================================================================

  RESTART POLICIES (auto-restart if the container stops/crashes)
  --------------------------------------------------------------------
  When running the container you can pass a `--restart` policy so Docker will
  automatically attempt to restart the container on failure or when the daemon
  restarts. Common choices:

  - Always restart unless explicitly stopped (recommended for services):
    $ docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name amj_exfinder_app --restart unless-stopped amj-exfinder:latest

  - Restart only on failures and limit retries (example: 5 retries):
    $ docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name amj_exfinder_app --restart on-failure:5 amj-exfinder:latest

  - Always keep running (even after Docker daemon restart):
    $ docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name amj_exfinder_app --restart always amj-exfinder:latest

  Choose `unless-stopped` or `always` for long-running services. Use `on-failure` when
  you want Docker to retry only when the process exits with a non-zero code.
